# Serene Pub - Development Configuration
# Copy this file to .env to customize development settings.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE GOES
#   For development (`npm run dev`), .env belongs in this repository root.
#
#   For a real install, it belongs in your DATA DIRECTORY, alongside the
#   database — not next to the executable:
#     Linux    ~/.local/share/SerenePub/.env
#     Windows  %APPDATA%/SerenePub/.env
#     macOS    ~/Library/Application Support/SerenePub/.env
#   or <SERENE_PUB_DATA_DIR>/.env when you've set that.
#
#   Updating Serene Pub replaces the install directory wholesale, so anything
#   kept there is destroyed on upgrade. A .env in the directory you launch from
#   is still read (and reported as deprecated at startup) — with one exception:
#   SERENE_PUB_DATA_DIR below chooses the data directory, so it can only come
#   from a real environment variable or from that install-directory file.
#
#   Real environment variables always win over both files.
#
# For the complete environment variable reference (including PORT, HOST,
# ORIGIN, NODE_ENV, and other adapter-node/hosting variables not listed
# below), see docs/environment-variables.md or docs/hosting.md.

# ===========================================
# APPLICATION DATA DIRECTORY
# ===========================================
# Override default data directory for development
# Default: Uses OS-appropriate directories
# Example for local development:
# SERENE_PUB_DATA_DIR=./dev-data

# ===========================================
# PUBLIC URL AND PROXY TRUST
# ===========================================
# These two are all a reverse-proxied or tunneled deployment normally needs.
# Everything in the DEPRECATED section further down is superseded by them.

# The address your users actually type, as a full origin including the
# scheme. Everything else is derived from it: HTTPS detection, session cookie
# Secure/SameSite flags, HSTS, the URL the browser opens its socket
# connection to, and SvelteKit's CSRF origin.
#
# Applied PER REQUEST, matched on hostname — so a request arriving on
# localhost:3000 still auto-detects plain http. One setting serves local and
# public access simultaneously, with nothing to flip between them.
#
# When your proxy serves /socket.io/ on this same origin, the socket endpoint
# is advertised with NO port, which is what makes Cloudflare Tunnel (and
# anything else that can't expose port 3001) work.
#
# Must be an origin, not a base path: "/serene" is rejected with a warning.
# PUBLIC_URL=https://serene.example.com

# Which addresses your reverse proxy connects from. Decides whether forwarded
# headers are believed at all, and setting it derives ADDRESS_HEADER,
# HOST_HEADER and PROTOCOL_HEADER for you.
#
# Accepts CIDRs, bare addresses, and the keywords `private` (loopback +
# RFC1918 + link-local; the default when unset), `none`, and `*`.
#
# Worth narrowing from the default: the app binds 0.0.0.0, so `private`
# trusts every host on your LAN to claim a client IP.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1

# ===========================================
# WEBSOCKET CONFIGURATION
# ===========================================
# By default the socket endpoint the browser connects to is auto-detected
# per-request from the page's own protocol/host — this is usually all you
# need, including for a reverse proxy/tunnel, AS LONG AS HOST_HEADER below is
# set so the app sees the real client-facing hostname.

# Extra hostnames allowed to open a socket connection, on top of the automatic
# default: any origin whose hostname matches the hostname the request itself
# arrived on. Socket.IO shares the app's own server, so an ordinary browser tab
# is same-origin and already covered — localhost, LAN IPs, and any custom domain
# or tunnel work with NO configuration at all. You only need this for the rare
# case where Origin and Host legitimately differ (a proxy rewriting Host).
# Comma-separated, hostname only (no scheme/port). Set to "*" to disable the
# allowlist entirely.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=example.com,192.168.1.50

# ===========================================
# ACCOUNT RECOVERY
# ===========================================
# Getting back into an instance when you are locked out. Setting these requires
# access to this file, which is the point: anyone who can edit it could already
# edit the database directly.

# First boot only — names and sets up the initial admin account. Both are
# ignored once that account has a password, so they cannot overwrite one later.
# SERENE_PUB_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# SERENE_PUB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=Choose-A-Strong-One!1

# First boot only — turns user accounts on automatically, for an unattended
# deployment. Only takes effect when SERENE_PUB_ADMIN_PASSWORD above is also set
# and valid: enabling accounts is permanent, so doing it without a working
# password would leave a login wall with no way through it. Off by default, and
# ignored entirely on an existing install or on Android (single-user by design).
# SERENE_PUB_ENABLE_ACCOUNTS=true

# Locked out of an existing instance? Set BOTH of these and restart.
#
# On the next boot the admin password is reset, two-factor authentication is
# cleared, every existing session is revoked, and the key is recorded as spent.
# Booting again with the SAME key does nothing — so these can sit here harmlessly
# instead of resetting your password on every restart. To reset again, choose a
# new key.
#
# The key is any string you pick; it is stored only as a hash. The password must
# meet the same rules the app's own UI enforces.
# SERENE_PUB_RECOVERY_KEY=any-string-you-choose
# SERENE_PUB_RECOVERY_PASSWORD=New-Strong-Password!1

# ===========================================
# CONTENT SECURITY POLICY
# ===========================================
# The app ships a strict-by-default CSP. These let you add extra allowed
# sources for content injected by your hosting layer that isn't part of the
# app itself — most commonly Cloudflare's "Browser Insights" beacon
# (static.cloudflareinsights.com), auto-injected into HTML responses when a
# zone is proxied through Cloudflare with that feature on. Prefer disabling
# such features in your CDN/proxy dashboard over widening these, since it's
# an injected script this app has no control over — but the escape hatch is
# here for cases where that isn't an option. Comma-separated.
# CSP_EXTRA_SCRIPT_SRC=https://static.cloudflareinsights.com
# CSP_EXTRA_STYLE_SRC=
# CSP_EXTRA_CONNECT_SRC=

# ===========================================
# REVERSE PROXY / TUNNEL (HTTPS termination in front of the app)
# ===========================================
# SETTING TRUSTED_PROXIES ABOVE FILLS ALL THREE OF THESE IN FOR YOU. They are
# here for setups whose proxy uses non-standard header names.
#
# By default adapter-node does NOT trust forwarded headers (Node sees every
# request as plain HTTP, since that's genuinely how the proxy talks to it).
# HOST_HEADER lets the app see the real client-facing hostname — which is also
# what the zero-config origin check compares against, so set it if your proxy
# rewrites Host. PROTOCOL_HEADER lets it see the real client-facing protocol;
# ADDRESS_HEADER the real client IP.
#
# Login rate limiting depends on ADDRESS_HEADER being correct behind a proxy —
# otherwise every user's failed logins share one bucket (the proxy's own
# address) and one person can lock everyone out. A claimed header is only
# honored when the request's direct peer passes TRUSTED_PROXIES, so a remote
# client can't spoof past the rate limiter, and it is safe to leave set on an
# install that is ALSO reached directly.
# PROTOCOL_HEADER=x-forwarded-proto
# HOST_HEADER=x-forwarded-host
# ADDRESS_HEADER=x-forwarded-for

# How many proxies adapter-node counts back through when reading
# X-Forwarded-For. Leave it alone — Serene Pub resolves the client address
# itself with depth-independent chain walking and never relies on this.
# XFF_DEPTH=1

# ===========================================
# SESSIONS
# ===========================================
# How long a login session lasts before it expires.
# Default: 168 (7 days)
# USER_TOKEN_EXPIRATION_HOURS=168

# ===========================================
# KOBOLDCPP MANAGED MODE
# ===========================================
# Only relevant when running KoboldCPP in Managed mode (Serene Pub downloads
# and runs the binary for you). KOBOLDCPP_BINARY_DIR points at a directory
# containing (or where an in-app download should place) the binary — only
# applied on first boot / if managed mode isn't already configured.
# KOBOLDCPP_BINARY_NAME points at a specific pre-existing binary filename
# inside that directory, for a binary you provided yourself.
# KOBOLDCPP_BINARY_DIR=/path/to/koboldcpp
# KOBOLDCPP_BINARY_NAME=koboldcpp-linux-x64

# ===========================================
# DEVELOPMENT DATABASE (External PostgreSQL)
# ===========================================
# These are only used when connecting to external PostgreSQL for development
# Production uses embedded PGlite automatically

# PostgreSQL connection for development
# DATABASE_PORT=5432
# POSTGRES_USER=postgres
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=password

# ===========================================
# DEVELOPMENT OPTIONS
# ===========================================
# Automatically open browser on startup (default: enabled)
# Set to 1 to disable automatic browser opening
# SERENE_AUTO_OPEN=1

# ===========================================
# COMMUNITY LIBRARY / EXTERNAL CARD SOURCES
# ===========================================
# Some external character card sources (eg. CharaVault) can optionally
# include NSFW/adult content in browse results. By default Serene Pub always
# excludes it and never surfaces the option to include it, regardless of
# what the connected CharaVault account's own settings allow. Setting this
# enables the "include NSFW" toggle in Character Library browsing (still off
# by default even once enabled) and allows outbound search requests to ask
# for NSFW-inclusive results when a user turns it on.
# Default: unset (NSFW browsing option is hidden entirely)
# ENABLE_UNSAFE_CHARACTER_BROWSING=true

# ===========================================
# ACCESSIBILITY / DOCUMENT VIEW
# ===========================================
# Document View is the simplified, high-contrast, keyboard- and
# screen-reader-friendly alternative UI (toggle in-app with Ctrl+Shift+Y).
# Setting this makes it the default for anyone who hasn't visited before —
# useful for an install primarily used by vision-impaired users. Only
# applies before a browser has its own stored preference: once someone has
# ever toggled Document View (on OR off) on a given browser, that choice is
# remembered and always wins over this setting, including if you change it
# later.
# Default: false (starts on the standard site)
# PUBLIC_DOCUMENT_VIEW_DEFAULT=true

# ===========================================
# DEPRECATED (still honored — nothing is broken)
# ===========================================
# Each of these is superseded by PUBLIC_URL / TRUSTED_PROXIES above. They
# continue to work indefinitely; Serene Pub prints a startup notice listing
# whichever ones you still have set, with the replacement for each. The old
# second-listener settings (SOCKETS_PORT, SOCKETS_ENDPOINT and friends) are not
# listed anywhere here — there is no second listener to point them at.
# See docs/hosting.md for the migration table.

# -> PUBLIC_URL=https://<hostname>
# Declares that this deployment terminates TLS itself rather than sitting
# behind a proxy that sets X-Forwarded-Proto.
# SERENE_PUB_SECURE_COOKIES=true
